WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:10.700
In ZOS Unix, pipes are a communication mechanism for sending arrays of data between two processes.

2
00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:13.700
Pipes are conceptually like a sequential file.

3
00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:18.700
Processes can write into a pipe and other processes can read from the pipe.

4
00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:21.700
Pipes exist only during the time they're open.

5
00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:27.700
The storage needed for the pipes is obtained from a data space associated with the kernel address space.

6
00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:29.700
And there are two types of pipes.

7
00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:34.700
Unnamed pipes are used between related processes, so for example between a parent and a child process.

8
00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:38.700
An unnamed pipe is created by the pipe function.

9
00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:41.700
The pipe function also opens the pipe to use.

10
00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:48.700
No security checking is performed on unnamed pipes since they are available only to a process and its children.

11
00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:51.700
And there's also named pipes which are also called FIFO.

12
00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:57.700
A FIFO resides in the hierarchical file system and can be referenced by a name.

13
00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:02.700
Thus, they can be used for communication between any two processes.

14
00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:07.700
Once processes are created, there are many ways to communicate between them.

15
00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:10.700
We've already talked about pipes being the most common way.

16
00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:13.700
Other ways include wait and execute.

17
00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:16.700
A parent process can wait on the exit of a child.

18
00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:18.700
Signals.

19
00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:21.700
One process can send a system or user signal to another.

20
00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:26.700
The receiving process should be coded to receive and process the signal.

21
00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:28.700
There's shared memory.

22
00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:34.700
Since processes run in different address spaces, these services can be used to define specific memory to be shared.

23
00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:39.700
The kernel will keep these pages in a shared data space.

24
00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:41.700
Message queues.

25
00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:49.700
This is a technique similar to piping but with the capability of reading and writing a message at a time rather than a string of characters.

26
00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:53.700
The kernel also uses a shared data space for these messages.

27
00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:58.700
And there's semaphores, which are used to control the use of shared memory by multiple processes.

28
00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:01.700
They are kept in the kernel process.

29
00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:08.700
The ZOS Unix interprocess communication or IPC functions are shared memory, message queues and semaphores.

30
00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:13.700
Users can invoke applications that create IPC resources and wait for IPC resources.

31
00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:18.700
IPC resources are not released when a process terminates or a user logs off.

32
00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:28.700
Therefore, it is possible that an IPC user might need assistance to remove an IPC resource using the Shell's ipcrm command.

33
00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:33.700
The ipcs shell command can be used to display the IPC resources in a system,

34
00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:39.700
which users own the resources and which users are waiting for a resource.

35
00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:47.700
Frequently used ZOS Unix programs can be placed into the ZOS link pack area or the LPA to improve performance.

36
00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:54.700
The Shell's a good candidate for that because only one copy of it can be used by all requesters, saving space in IO.

37
00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:59.700
When searching for a program with the sticky bit on, ZOS Unix will look in the user's step line,

38
00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:03.700
the link pack area and the link list concatenation.

39
00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:08.700
In order to keep a ZOS Unix program in, LPA, a number of things must be done.

40
00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:15.700
The file in the hierarchical file system that contains the program must have the sticky bit set by an authorized user.

41
00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:18.700
The sticky bit can also be set on a directory level.

42
00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:24.700
It limits removing or renaming subdirectories or files to the owner or the super user.

43
00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:31.700
Also, the program must be link edited and placed in sys1.lpalib or a dataset in LPA list.

44
00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:36.700
The program is copied to LPA during the next IPL.

45
00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:42.700
Threads provide support for multiple separate units of dispatchable work within a process.

46
00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:47.700
A ZOS Unix thread can be compared with a ZOS task.

47
00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:52.700
The thread support allows application programmers to develop multitasking server applications

48
00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:56.700
that require multiple concurrent execution streams.

49
00:03:56.700 --> 00:04:02.700
A program using threads can have significant performance benefits in a multiprocessor environment

50
00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:07.700
where each thread in the process can run on an individual processor.

51
00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:13.700
Threads are created with the pthread underscore create c function.

52
00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:18.700
Since threads share address space, there are services to protect the memory of the address space

53
00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:21.700
and to synchronize the execution of the threads.

54
00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:26.700
These are mutex, which is a structure associated with the variable,

55
00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:30.700
and used by threads to determine if a variable is in use,

56
00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:36.700
and condition variable used to synchronize the execution of the activities of the threads.

57
00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:41.700
So what are some of the differences between ZOS tasks and POSIX threads?

58
00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:44.700
ZOS has a task hierarchy.

59
00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:49.700
POSIX threads have no hierarchy except for the initial thread which behaves differently from the other threads.

60
00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:52.700
ZOS tasks usually run different programs.

61
00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:56.700
All POSIX threads in a process run the same program.

62
00:04:56.700 --> 00:04:59.700
ZOS tasks can be independent or cooperating.

63
00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:03.700
POSIX threads must cooperate, they do not operate independently.

64
00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:09.700
ZOS allocates storage at task level, and some storage can be shared between tasks and some not.

65
00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:13.700
The programs can be located in common storage, CSA or LPA,

66
00:05:13.700 --> 00:05:19.700
in private area below the 16 megabyte line, or in private area above the 16 megabyte line.

67
00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:24.700
All POSIX threads in a process share all storage allocated to the process.

68
00:05:24.700 --> 00:05:28.700
Now, this diagram shows the ZOS program management functions

69
00:05:28.700 --> 00:05:32.700
and the equivalent functions in ZOS Unix.

70
00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:36.700
Some of these similarities include that programs can reside in different libraries

71
00:05:36.700 --> 00:05:40.700
and there is an automatic search to locate them.

72
00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:46.700
Both interfaces provide a method to replace the current executing program with a new program.

73
00:05:46.700 --> 00:05:51.700
And both sets of interfaces have ways to create work in separate address spaces.

74
00:05:51.700 --> 00:05:59.700
But there are also many differences, including with tasks, load and program execution.

75
00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:03.700
And that's it for ZOS Unix functions.

76
00:06:03.700 --> 00:06:08.700
In the next module, we'll look at working with the ZOS Unix environment.
